scouts.summary:
@tjrobertson52 is TJ Robertson, founder of TJ Digital (tjrobertson.com), a small agency that sells AI-search optimisation, SEO and Google Ads to small and medium businesses. That is marketing, not sales: none of the four captions of his that I saw is about closing or outreach. What we can learn from him comes in four parts. (1) He positions the agency on transparency: it spends clients' money as its own and shows where every dollar goes. (2) He places content on Medium, LinkedIn and Reddit so a small brand can outrank bigger ones. (3) He optimises for AI answer engines (ChatGPT and Google AI results). (4) He uses AI to turn one piece of content into many. He also opens videos with alarmist, contrarian hooks ("Digital marketing will be DEAD by 2027"). We should copy the structure of those hooks, not the fear.

Selling on TikTok as a whole (small sample: two searches limited to tiktok.com, 20 results) is dominated by live selling: cold calling (@underdogsales) and high-ticket remote closing with objection scripts (@geoffketterer). Those conflict with the owner's rule never to talk to customers, and I list them only so they are not silently dropped. The part we can use is written selling:
- value first, "make a deposit before an ask" (@salestipstok);
- what every sales email and sales letter must contain (@russellbrunson; the contents are not in the caption);
- a short sales page: pain point, then solution, then credentials, then the call to action (@copyfolioapp);
- a five-step copy loop: hook, understand, keep, edit, call to act (@learncopywritingnow);
- email subject lines (@teachable.com).

These map straight onto our model. The free PCN874 validator, the free calculators and the free MCP server are the "deposit"; the Pro licence is the "ask". The rest become a pricing page and FAQ that answer objections ahead of time, credentials a faceless brand can prove (spec citations, open code, test counts; never fake reviews), and emails sent only to people who opted in. Cold email is not safe: Israel's anti-spam law (section 30A) probably rules out unsolicited commercial email, and that is unverified.

Evidence limits:
- Every TikTok claim here comes from a search-result title or snippet. Captions are not what the videos teach: the actual lessons are in the audio, which I did not hear.
- A curl from this container to the TikTok oEmbed endpoint and to tjrobertson.com both failed with CONNECT 403.
- Follower and like counts are snippet grade only.
